)`. We call straight\n42\t    /// into the platform C library so that the bytes emitted, and the way they\n43\t    /// interleave with any stdio buffering performed by the caller, match the\n44\t    /// original library exactly (a Rust `println!` would use a separate,\n45\t    /// independently flushed buffer).\n46\t    fn printf(format: *const c_char, ...) -> c_int;\n47\t}\n48\t\n49\t/// `\"%s\\n\"` format string, NUL terminated, matching the C source literal.\n50\tstatic FMT_S_NEWLINE: [c_char; 4] = [b'%' as c_char, b's' as c_char, b'\\n' as c_char, 0];\n51\t\n52\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n53\t// void printLine(const char *line)\n54\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n55\t//\n56\t//     void printLine(const char *line)\n57\t//     {\n58\t//         if (line != NULL)\n59\t//         {\n60\t//             printf(\"%s\\n\", line);\n61\t//         }\n62\t//     }\n63\t\n64\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n65\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn printLine(line: *const c_char) {\n66\t    if !line.is_null() {\n67\t        printf(FMT_S_NEWLINE.as_ptr(), line);\n68\t    }\n69\t}\n70\t\n71\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n72\t// static char *helperBad()\n73\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n74\t//\n75\t//     static char *helperBad()\n76\t//     {\n77\t//         char charString[] = \"helperBad string\";\n78\t//         return charString;\n79\t//     }\n80\t//\n81\t// This is the CWE-562 (\"Return of Stack Variable Address\") defect the original\n82\t// sample exists to demonstrate: `charString` is an automatic array whose\n83\t// lifetime ends when `helperBad` returns, so returning it yields a dangling\n84\t// pointer and the behaviour is undefined.\n85\t//\n86\t// The bug is deliberately NOT fixed here. It is reproduced with the exact\n87\t// observable behaviour of the compiled C library: GCC diagnoses the return of a\n88\t// local address (`-Wreturn-local-addr`) and emits `mov $0x0, %eax` for the\n89\t// return value, i.e. `helperBad` hands back a NULL pointer. Verified on the\n90\t// reference build at both -O0 and -O2:\n91\t//\n92\t//     000000000000115b <helperBad>:\n93\t//       ...\n94\t//       117f:  mov    $0x0,%eax        # returns NULL\n95\t//       1184:  pop    %rbp\n96\t//       1185:  ret\n97\t//\n98\t// Consequently `bad()` calls `printLine(NULL)`, which prints nothing at all --\n99\t// the string \"helperBad string\" never reaches the output. Returning a real\n100\t// pointer to a local buffer here (or, worse, a pointer to a static/leaked copy)\n101\t// would print a line the C library does not print and would break byte-for-byte\n102\t// output equivalence.\n103\tfn helperBad() -> *mut c_char {\n104\t    // The local `charString` is materialised exactly as the C compiler does\n105\t    // (the bytes are built up in the frame) and then discarded, because the\n106\t    // dead store is not observable and the returned value is NULL.\n107\t    let _charString: [c_char; 17] = {\n108\t        let mut buf = [0 as c_char; 17];\n109\t        let src = b\"helperBad string\\0\";\n110\t        let mut i = 0;\n111\t        while i < src.len() {\n112\t            buf[i] = src[i] as c_char;\n113\t            i += 1;\n114\t        }\n115\t        buf\n116\t    };\n117\t\n118\t    core::ptr::null_mut()\n119\t}\n120\t\n121\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n122\t// void bad()\n123\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n124\t//\n125\t//     void bad()\n126\t//     {\n127\t//         printLine(helperBad());\n128\t//     }\n129\t\n130\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n131\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn bad() {\n132\t    printLine(helperBad());\n133\t}\n134\t\n135\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n136\t// static char *helperGood1()\n137\t// ---