{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_timp2xj3m3xnwgz6ruo4pkamr3xxj365qmqq4ymgctmy5byvvuqa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. **No divergence found — the Rust translation is byte-for-byte equivalent to the C.**\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is one 68-line translation unit exporting 4 symbols: `printLine`, `bad`, `good`, `driver`. It's a CWE-562 (\"Return of Stack Variable Address\") demonstration.\n\n## Phase A — artifacts (all in `translation/`)\n\n| file | contents |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | All 4 C exports mapped to the Rust `.so`; **0 missing**, sets exactly equal |\n| `ERRORS.md` | 3 real rejection branches (the C has exactly one null check, 0 asserts, 0 error codes) + 11 mandated FFI-boundary rows |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | 22 option × input-shape combinations across all 4 entry points |\n| `FEATURES.md` | Feature/profile matrix + mutation-sensitivity results |\n| `VERIFICATION.md` | The completion gate |\n\n## Phases B/C/D — results\n\n**204 passing cases** (51 per run × 2 feature combos × 2 profiles). Randomized rows run 200–2000 seeded inputs each. Tests load *both* `.so`s via `libloading` and compare captured fd-1 bytes; no Rust function is called directly.\n\nI also added `tests/optlevels.rs`, which recompiles the untouched C at `-O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os` into `$TMPDIR` and re-runs the battery — because the translation's correctness hinges on how the compiler resolves UB (see below).\n\n## Two things worth flagging\n\n**1. The load-bearing fact, now proven rather than assumed.** `helperBad()` returns the address of its automatic array. The Rust translation asserted in a comment that GCC resolves this to `mov $0x0,%eax` (returns NULL) → `printLine`'s guard suppresses output → **`bad()` and `driver(0)` print nothing**. I confirmed this in the disassembly and, more importantly, showed it holds at every optimization level. A mutation \"fixing\" the defect is caught by 17 cases.\n\n**2. The one real bug I found was in my own harness, not the translation.** Since the only observable behavior is fd-1 output, and capturing it means redirecting fd 1 process-wide, libtest's *parallel progress lines* were landing inside the captured bytes:\n\n```\nC = helperGood1 string\\ntest c14_bad_repeated ...\nRust = helperGood1 string\\n\n```\n\nThose were spurious. The differential suites now use `harness = false` with a sequential runner that flushes Rust's `io::stdout()` before each capture.\n\nTo guard against the opposite failure mode — vacuously passing tests — I ran a mutation battery. All six injected defects were caught by multiple cases each, and each was caught by the row written for that behavior. `src/lib.rs` is back to its verified state; the only edits I made to it were `# Safety` doc comments and one comment update (zero behavioral change).\n\n**Caveat on scope:** `printLine` with a non-NULL *invalid* pointer is UB with no defined result to compare, so it's documented as out of scope in `ERRORS.md` rather than silently skipped."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1451,"cache_read_input_tokens":138936,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1451,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b6a4d4fe-b39f-49eb-8eeb-f9032072843f","uuid":"97d4e60f-8618-493d-8c89-3d8a8b304d71","timestamp":"2026-08-28T12:53:40.376Z"}
